analysis:
The only change in this PR is documentation in docs/architecture/inference.md, head lines 1030-1048. It adds a paragraph describing PromptTokenCache, ChatTemplateLoopRewrites and the per-request token-ID reuse path in LocalTokenizerBridge. Unit 9bceb6d953b336e2:9 (head lines 416-797) and unit 9bceb6d953b336e2:10 (head lines 798-1209) are unchanged context apart from that paragraph. No code changed, so no security control was altered in this PR.

The documented behavior touches A-001 (consumer prompts) and relates to T-041 (cross-tenant prefix-cache sharing and TTFT timing oracle). The doc states that "Reuse never crosses a cache scope" and that reuse is partitioned by the coordinator-authored cache scope. That is consistent with T-041's scope-isolation mitigation. It also states that the cache is bypassed when no scope exists, which matches the earlier statement that coordinator text prefix reuse is off without a scope.

The documented token-ID cache is in-process and is not a persistent artifact. It does not add on-disk state, so it does not affect T-041 leak #2 or the SSD tier's TTL and KEK posture. Faster TTFT for repeated pieces within a scope could widen the same-account timing oracle already accepted as SEC-035. This is a minor possibility and is not evidenced by any code in this diff.

Unverified contracts: the implementation in PromptTokenCache.swift and LocalTokenizerBridge was not supplied. The following claims therefore cannot be verified here: that cache keys really are scoped by the authenticated cacheScope, that the background comparison actually disables reuse on mismatch, and that memory is bounded. A later pass that sees the code should confirm the following:
- cache keys include the coordinator-authored scope;
- prompt-derived pieces are not logged or persisted;
- the cache has eviction bounds, to limit DoS from adversarial consumers;
- the multi-core encoding (up to four cores) is bounded.

I found no concrete regression in the documentation text itself.
